{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CfezNjoiYGh3k5JLxtdtv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe length check lands in the right unit. `OwnerTelephones` is already the single home for telephone normalization, so extending it with the national-number rule keeps all the E.164 knowledge in one place rather than scattering it into a new class or into the function pipeline.\n\n- The new logic is factored into a focused private helper `hasValidNationalLength(...)` with a clear name and intent-revealing Javadoc, rather than being inlined into `toE164`. `toE164` stays a readable sequence of guard clauses (`< 8 || > 15`, then `!hasValidNationalLength`), which reads naturally.\n- Expressing the rule as `digits.length() - 2 == 9` / `digits.length() - 1 == 10` (national digits) mirrors the spec's wording precisely, so a maintainer reads the code and the spec the same way. The \"unrecognized country code falls back to the general rule\" behavior is stated in the Javadoc, so the `return true` default isn't a silent surprise.\n- For two country codes an `if`-chain is the idiomatic, right-sized choice; a lookup table would be premature. Nothing is duplicated — the country-code facts restated in the exception message and Javadocs are documentation, not logic.\n- The exception message and both Javadocs were updated in step with the behavior change, so the class contracts stay honest. No class or method is turning into a catch-all."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10123,"cache_read_input_tokens":11894,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10123},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"092c6879-3718-4c4e-b575-fa521